course: politics
lesson: 5
title: President and Prime Minister, White House and Downing Street
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The USA has a president, chosen by voters in a separate election, who is both head of the country and head of the government; the UK has a prime minister, the leader of the party that wins most seats in Parliament, while the King is the head of state.
explain: Each top job has a famous address: the US president lives and works in the White House, with an egg-shaped office called the Oval Office, and the UK prime minister lives and works at 10 Downing Street, behind a black door with a big number 10.
explain: Neither leader works alone: each picks a cabinet, a top team of ministers who run things like health, schools and money; a cabinet is a team, not a cupboard.
explain: Neither can do anything they like either: a US president is checked by Congress and the courts and can be elected only twice, and a UK prime minister must face noisy questions from MPs every Wednesday and can be replaced by their own party without a general election.
explain: The UK prime minister is also an ordinary MP for one local area and must win that seat at each election, like a head teacher who still teaches one class.
fact: A US president is elected by voters in a separate election and is head of the country and of the government.
fact: In the UK, the prime minister is the leader of the party that wins most seats in Parliament, and the King is head of state.
fact: 10 Downing Street in London, often called Number 10, is the prime minister's office and official home; new prime ministers usually give a speech outside its black door.
fact: The White House in Washington DC is where the US president lives and works; the Oval Office is the president's oval-shaped office.
fact: The cabinet is the top team of ministers; a minister is in charge of one area, like health or education, and the leader chooses who is in it.
fact: Prime Minister's Questions (PMQs) happens every Wednesday when Parliament is sitting; MPs, including the leader of the opposition, question the prime minister in public.
fact: The opposition is the parties not in government; their job is to challenge it.
fact: A US president can be elected only twice, for four years each time; this is a term limit; UK prime ministers have no fixed limit but must keep winning elections and keep their party's support.
fact: In the UK, if a prime minister resigns, their party can choose a new leader who becomes prime minister without a general election; this happened several times between 2016 and 2022.
fact: Canada, Australia and New Zealand also have the British king as head of state, but a governor general stands in for him and the prime minister and parliament make the real decisions.
fact: In the US, power is split between the president, Congress (which makes laws) and the courts; each can block the others in some ways, which is called checks and balances.
fact: After a UK general election, the winning party's leader goes to the King, who asks them to form a government; this is a tradition, and the King does not choose the winner.
fact: A UK prime minister is also an MP for one area, called a constituency; a US president does not represent any local area.
exercise: Mateo has a president and his London cousin has a prime minister. Which is true? | Both lead their governments, but they are chosen in different ways | Only the president is a real leader | Only the prime minister is a real leader | They are two names for exactly the same job
exercise: Ruby sees the prime minister outside a black door with a 10 on it. Where is that? | 10 Downing Street in London | The White House in Washington | Buckingham Palace in London | The Houses of Parliament
exercise: Tyler sees the White House from the fence. What is it? | The home and office of the US president | The home of the British king | The building where US votes are counted | A museum about old presidents only
exercise: What is the Oval Office? | The US president's oval-shaped office | The room where all votes are posted | The prime minister's kitchen in London | A shop that sells eggs in Washington
exercise: Aisha hears the prime minister "has a new cabinet". What is it? | The top team of ministers who help run the government | A new cupboard where the prime minister keeps important papers | A group of judges who choose the laws | A new kind of polling station
exercise: Dylan sees MPs shouting "Hear, hear!" while the prime minister answers questions. What is that? | Prime Minister's Questions, where MPs question the leader in public | A school assembly held inside Parliament | A protest by people who lost the election | A party to celebrate the prime minister's birthday
exercise: Why do people say PMQs matters, even though it is noisy? | The leader has to face tough questions in public every week | It is the only time in the week that MPs are allowed to speak | It decides who wins the next election | It is when the King chooses the new laws
exercise: Amira learns a US president can only serve two terms. Why? | To stop one person keeping power for too long | Because presidents get too tired after eight years | Because the White House needs repainting | Because voters are only allowed to vote twice
exercise: What do people who dislike term limits say? | If voters keep wanting someone, they should be allowed to choose them again | Presidents should only be allowed one day in office | Term limits make elections happen every week | Nobody has ever argued against term limits
exercise: The UK got a new prime minister on a day with no election. How? | The old one resigned and the party chose a new leader | The King simply picked his favourite MP all by himself | The new one won a running race all round Downing Street | The old prime minister just handed the job to a relative
exercise: Fatima in Canada asks why her prime minister talks about the King. Why? | The King is also Canada's head of state, though Canadians run their own government | Canada is run from London by the King every day | The King is Canada's prime minister as well | Canada has to ask the King before every law
exercise: Who stands in for the King in Canada, Australia and New Zealand? | A governor general | A local head teacher | A senator from Washington | The mayor of the capital city
exercise: Zara asks if the president can do anything he wants. What stops him? | Congress and the courts can check him, under the rules | Nothing at all; a president can do absolutely anything he likes | Only the king of Britain is allowed to stop a US president | His cabinet can fire him whenever it likes, without a reason
exercise: Why does the prime minister go to see the King after an election? | It is the tradition: the King asks the winner to form a government | The King chooses who won the election by looking at the candidates | The King must check the prime minister's homework | The prime minister must hand over the votes
exercise: Chloe learns the prime minister is still an MP for one area. What does that mean? | They must win that local seat and still represent its people | They no longer have to be elected by anyone once they are leader | They run that area's schools by themselves | They must live in that area's town hall
exercise: What is the opposition? | The parties not in government, whose job is to challenge it | A group of people who are against all elections of any kind | The team that counts all the votes in secret after an election | The prime minister's closest friends, who give advice in private
test: Mateo's cousin in Texas says, "Y'all's prime minister ain't a real boss, our president's the real deal." What is the fair answer? | Both lead governments with real power, chosen in different ways and checked by others | He's right, prime ministers have no power at all | He's right, and presidents can do anything they like | A prime minister is just the king's helper with no job
test: A Gen Z kid on TikTok says, "PMQs is literally a roast battle, it's giving chaos." ("It's giving" means it looks like.) What is the serious point of it? | The leader has to answer questions in public every week | It is a comedy show where MPs compete to win prizes and cups | It is where MPs decide which people get sent to prison that week | Nothing; it has no purpose at all, it is just for the cameras
test: Your little brother says, "The prime minister lives at Buckingham Palace, innit." Put him right. | The prime minister's official home and office is 10 Downing Street | He's right, the King and the prime minister share rooms at the palace | The prime minister lives in the White House | The prime minister has no home and sleeps in Parliament
test: An American grandpa says, "That president was rad, they should let him go round three!" What is the rule? | A US president can only be elected twice | A president can be elected as many times as voters like | A president must stand down after one year | Only grandpas can vote for a third term
test: Your sister says, "No election, but a new PM? That's well dodgy, they must've cheated." ("Dodgy" means suspicious.) What is true? | In the UK the governing party can choose a new leader, who becomes PM | She's right, it's always cheating | The King picks a brand new PM all by himself at the start of every year | The old PM handed it to a family member
test: What would happen if a president had no Congress or courts to check him? | One person could make rules without anyone able to stop bad ones | Laws would be checked twice as carefully, since he would do it himself | Voters would have more say than before | Nothing would change in how the country is run
test: A Canadian kid says, "The King's on our money, eh, but our PM runs the show." Is that right? | Yes, the King is head of state, but Canada's government makes the decisions | No, the King runs Canada's government day to day | No, Canada has never had any link to the King | No, the PM is just the King's driver
test: A video game shows the White House as the British king's castle. What should be fixed? | The White House is the US president's home and office | The White House is where the prime minister sleeps | The White House belongs to the Canadian governor general | Nothing, the British king lives there in summer
test: Aisha, being cheeky, says, "A cabinet? So the PM's got a cupboard of mates." What's the real meaning? | The top team of ministers who run big areas like health and schools | A cupboard where the prime minister keeps files | A secret club of friends that meets in the kitchen at Downing Street | The leader's family members who live with them
test: A pal from Newcastle asks, "Hoo can the PM be the boss and still be wor local MP?" ("Hoo" means how; "wor" means our.) What is the answer? | In the UK the prime minister is also an MP who must win a local seat | They give up being an MP as soon as they become PM | Only US presidents represent a local area | Prime ministers are never elected anywhere
test: Dad, very formally, says, "The Leader of His Majesty's Most Loyal Opposition shall now put the question." Who is that? | The leader of the biggest party not in government | The King's own personal secretary at Buckingham Palace | The prime minister's oldest and best friend in the cabinet | A judge who keeps order in the courtroom during trials
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between a President of the United States and a Prime Minister of the United Kingdom? | The former is elected in a separate ballot as head of state and of government; the latter leads the party commanding a majority in Parliament | The former is appointed by the British monarch; the latter is elected by the citizens of every state | Both offices are hereditary and pass from parent to child | Neither office is subject to any form of election
exam: State the maximum number of occasions on which a person may be elected to the presidency of the United States. | Two | One | Three | There is no maximum
exam: It is given that a Prime Minister of the United Kingdom has resigned between general elections. Identify the customary means by which a successor is determined. | The selection of a new leader by the governing party | A nationwide ballot held on the following morning | The personal preference of the monarch, freely exercised | The appointment of the most senior judge
exam: Identify the principal function of Prime Minister's Questions. | The public examination of the head of government by Members of Parliament | The selection of candidates for the forthcoming election | The formal signature of bills by the sovereign in the presence of Parliament | The counting of ballot papers in the presence of observers
